A||N||6||fropuff||What could this be?||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||03-30-2001||05:21 PM||||Hey, there's a weird scraping noise on the left rear wheel when I take a right turn.  I know that it is something coming in contact with the rotor, but I can't find what it is.  The rear brakes get hot after a drive; do Fieros have rear brakes as well as front ones?  They get REALLY hot.  Sounds like a brake pad is dragging, but I don't think it is.  What could it be?  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steven||03-30-2001||05:27 PM||||There is a little metal clip that holds the inboard pad to the piston of the caliper. You might want to look at that. If the clip has rusted away or just broke with age, you might have the inboard pad sliding back and forth in the caliper just enough to make a rubbing/draging/grinding sound.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 03-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||03-30-2001||05:29 PM||||If it is this, what do I do to fix it?  I will go check it out now.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||03-30-2001||05:45 PM||||Well, I think it's got something to do with the brake pads.  That little wear indicator on the pad was coming into contact with the rotor when rounding a curve.  I will get new pads and see what happens.  Thanks a lot.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||03-30-2001||05:47 PM||||You would have to replace that clip. To describe the clip, if memory serves, it was very thin round in shape, and 4 little tines that held on to the outer edge of the shoe. It also lock in to the piston, but I can't remember how. When I bought my Fiero, the rear brake sucked. Someone told me that the front calipers would mount on the rear if you cris crossed them LF to RR and RF to LR. So I bought two rebuilt caliper for $17.99 ea and bolted them on. I also had rear rotors turn to be .440" which is still within spec. That was about 4 1/2 years ago. No problems yet, except I don't have a park brake. Which was ok with me. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||03-30-2001||08:14 PM||||Brakes are nothing to fool with if you don't know what you're doing. Your and possibly others lives depend on them. Bite the bullet and have a knowledgeable person fix them. It's not a very difficult job but learn how to do it properly ||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||03-30-2001||08:25 PM||||or perhaps the dust sheild is making contact with the rotor - send a long extension through the wheel and give it a tap.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
